package pages

templ SyntaxUnionsPage() {
	<h1>Unions</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>Unions in ghoti act very similar to tagged unions in other languages</li>
		<li>
			Unions are defined using the standard declaration syntax or with a <code>using</code> statement
			<ul>
				<li>In the event that the standard declaration syntax is used, it must be <code>const</code></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			Unions can be annotated with layout modifiers:
			<ul>
				<li><code>extern union { "{ ... }" }</code>: Enforces C ABI union layout rules for C interop.</li>
				<li>
					As with <code>extern</code> structs, an <code>extern</code> union field may not contain a reference or a <code>dyn</code> fat pointer, transitively through pointers, arrays, slices, or function-pointer signatures. Use a raw pointer <code>^T</code> instead
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>packed union { "{ ... }" }</code>: A <em>bit-packed</em> union. The whole union becomes a single backing integer <code>iN</code> whose width <code>N</code> is the width of its widest field (<code>max { "@bitSizeOf" }(field)</code>); every field is laid out at bit offset 0, so writing one field overwrites the low bits the others share.
					<ul>
						<li>Field eligibility, the 128-bit cap, the <code>{ "@alignas" }</code> ban, and the <code>&amp;u.field</code> address error all match a bit-packed <code>packed struct</code>.</li>
						<li>A bit-packed union carries no active-field tag &mdash; it is a raw reinterpreting view, like a C union. <code>{ "@bitSizeOf" }(U)</code> is <code>N</code>; <code>{ "@sizeOf" }(U)</code> is the backing integer's ABI byte size.</li>
						<li><code>extern packed union { "{ ... }" }</code> keeps C-ABI byte semantics and is not bit-packed.</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			A union is defined by a a comma separated list of key-value pairs, where:
			<ul>
				<li>Each key is an identifier which is used for the active tag</li>
				<li>Each value is a type for said identifier</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Only one field of a union can be active at one time</li>
		<li>Attempting to access an inactive field of a union is safety-checked</li>
		<li>
			A union field's identifier can be used to check if it is active by using the equality operator and implicit access expression
			<ul>
				<li>You can also use this to match over an union, capturing the active fields value with a capture clause</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>The inner types of a union are not restricted to any subset or superset of the language (i.e. you are not restricted to only primitive types nor are you prohibited from using user-defined types)</li>
		<li>Initializing a union is done through the exact same mechanism as structs, using the <code>Name{ "{" }.field = value{ "}" }</code> syntax (shorthand <code>.{ "{" }.field = value{ "}" }</code>)</li>
		<li>To access a union's members, the <code>.</code> operator is used</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const Hand := union {
    card: bool,
    pen: i32,
    shoe: void,
};

var h1 := Hand{ .card = true }; // Initializes h1 to have the card field active
var h2: Hand = .{ .card = true }; // Equivalent to h1

if (h1 == Hand.card) { ... }; // Checks if the card field is active
if (h1 == .card) { ... }; // Equivalent to above

match (h1) {
    .card => |c| { ... } // Capture the boolean field if active
    .pen => |_| { ... } // Discarded
    .shoe => { ... } // No capture
};` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>
			You can put declarations inside of an union as you would with a struct
			<ul>
				<li>Member functions have a first argument which is an instance of the enum as explained in the struct documentation</li>
				<li>There is no concept of an instance field in an enum</li>
				<li>These members must appear after all fields</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Union 'members' can also be <code>using</code> or <code>import</code> statements</li>
	</ul>
}
